WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.780
Removing a protein can cause a defect.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.820
But that alone does not show that losing this protein caused it.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.260
The treatment may affect something else.

4
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.900
Use independent controls, then put the protein back.

5
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.560
If the defect goes away, rescue is strong evidence that losing the intended protein

6
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.420
caused it. This matters because finding a protein in the right place at the right

7
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.060
time does not show that it causes anything.

8
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.820
These experiments test different questions.

9
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:38.183
A short hairpin RNA can reduce a protein by targeting its messenger RNA.

10
00:00:39.283 --> 00:00:43.543
Use another hairpin against a different region, and check that the protein is lost.

11
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:48.963
For rescue, silent mutations change the messenger but keep the protein the same.

12
00:00:49.803 --> 00:00:51.683
The hairpin cannot pair with this version.

13
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:55.983
Check that the rescue protein is made at about its normal level.

14
00:00:56.967 --> 00:00:58.627
Now ask which job matters.

15
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.467
Depleting clathrin prolonged mitosis.

16
00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:06.607
Was that a spindle defect, or an indirect effect of losing endocytosis?

17
00:01:07.627 --> 00:01:10.067
Full-length resistant clathrin rescued mitosis.

18
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:15.667
A shortened version partly rescued mitosis while failing to support transferrin

19
00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.087
uptake, the readout of endocytosis.

20
00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:23.127
Keeping part of one function while losing the other argues that the jobs are

21
00:01:23.127 --> 00:01:27.667
separate. Score both functions, rather than deciding from location alone.

22
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.200
A different question needs different evidence.

23
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.940
In what order does secretory protein visit compartments?

24
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.320
A short pulse of radioactive amino acid labels one batch.

25
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.180
A chase with unlabelled amino acid lets you follow it.

26
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.300
In pancreatic slices, label appeared first in ER fragments, then in Golgi fractions,

27
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.100
then in the granule fraction.

28
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.920
That gives an order.

29
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:58.220
It does not choose between vesicles budding and fusing, and compartments maturing

30
00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:59.220
into one another.

31
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:02.073
Finally, where does an enzyme live?

32
00:02:02.913 --> 00:02:07.633
In the lesson's made-up fractionation table, enzyme X follows the whole acid

33
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:11.113
phosphatase profile, rather than the mitochondrial marker.

34
00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:13.913
That makes X provisionally lysosomal.

35
00:02:14.853 --> 00:02:17.953
Enrichment is not purity: fractions contain mixtures.

36
00:02:18.953 --> 00:02:22.173
Activity revealed by opening a membrane supports enclosure.

37
00:02:23.113 --> 00:02:28.273
A second lysosomal marker on a density gradient, and imaging in intact cells, would

38
00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:29.533
help confirm the assignment.
